  "prediction": "**Aegis Ballistic Missile Defense (BMD) Program: Background and Issues for Congress**\n\nThe Aegis BMD program is a critical component of the United States' ballistic missile defense (BMD) capabilities, providing a capability for conducting BMD operations on Navy Aegis cruisers and destroyers. The program is carried out by the Missile Defense Agency (MDA) and the Navy, with funding provided through MDA's budget and the Navy's budget. The Aegis BMD system has undergone significant upgrades and improvements over the years, with the latest variants capable of intercepting intercontinental ballistic missiles (ICBMs).\n\nThe Aegis BMD system uses the Standard Missile-3 (SM-3) interceptor, which is designed to intercept ballistic missiles above the atmosphere (exo-atmospheric intercept) in the midcourse phase of an enemy ballistic missile's flight. The SM-3 has undergone several upgrades, including the SM-3 Block IIA, which has a larger-diameter kinetic warhead and improved propulsion. The SM-3 Block IIA has shown great promise in recent testing and is being considered for use against ICBMs.\n\nThe Aegis BMD program has undergone several phases, with the European Phased Adaptive Approach (EPAA) being the first application of the approach in Europe. The EPAA involves using BMD-capable Aegis ships, a land-based radar in Europe, and two Aegis Ashore sites in Romania and Poland to defend Europe against ballistic missile threats from countries such as Iran.\n\nThe Navy's Chief of Naval Operations, Adm. John Richardson, has expressed concerns about the burden that BMD operations are placing on the Navy's fleet of Aegis ships, particularly since performing BMD patrols requires those ships to operate in geographic locations that may be unsuitable for performing other U.S. Navy missions. Richardson has suggested that the Navy should consider a more persistent, land-based solution for BMD operations, such as establishing more Aegis Ashore sites.\n\nThe Aegis BMD program faces several challenges, including technical risk and test and evaluation issues. The Director, Operational Test and Evaluation (DOT&E) has identified several areas for improvement, including ensuring that ground tests of all SM-3 missile components use the same configuration as will be flown in flight tests, determining how to properly score acceptance ground test data for production missiles, and funding and executing high-fidelity modeling and simulation (M&S) for Aegis BL 9.2 SM-3 Block IIA and SM-6 Dual II scenarios.\n\nCongress",
